<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">stand</ansi>

The <ansi fg="command">stand</ansi> command forces you to your feet when you've been knocked
down (<ansi fg="red">prone</ansi>). It's guaranteed to succeed, but costs a meaningful
chunk of stamina.

<ansi fg="yellow">Requirements:</ansi>

  - Must be prone (knocked down)
  - Must have enough stamina to pay the cost

<ansi fg="yellow">Usage:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">stand</ansi>
  Stand up immediately, bypassing automatic recovery

<ansi fg="yellow">Mechanics:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="stat">Stamina Cost:</ansi> A meaningful portion of your maximum stamina
  <ansi fg="stat">Success Rate:</ansi> Guaranteed. You spend the effort and get up
    regardless of who is holding you down
  <ansi fg="stat">Action Cost:</ansi> Uses your action for the current round

<ansi fg="yellow">Automatic Recovery:</ansi>

If you don't manually stand, you will <ansi fg="yellow">automatically attempt</ansi> to
recover each round after a brief delay:

  - <ansi fg="stat">Minimum Duration:</ansi> Must remain prone for at least one full round
  - <ansi fg="stat">Recovery Chance:</ansi> A free stand is a contest against whoever is
    holding you down. If nobody is attacking you, you simply
    stand back up
  - <ansi fg="stat">Recovery Penalty:</ansi> Only one attack that round (your focus is on
    getting up)

<ansi fg="yellow">Strategy:</ansi>

Use <ansi fg="command">stand</ansi> when you must get up immediately and have stamina to
spare. Otherwise, save your stamina and let automatic recovery handle
it. You still get one attack while scrambling up.

If you are sleeping, <ansi fg="command">stand</ansi> will wake you up.

